# Cross-platform line-ending policy.
#
# Why this exists: several test suites assert on the SOURCE TEXT of shipped
# files (see test/auth-action.test.js, test/technical-seo-foundation.test.js).
# With core.autocrlf=true and no policy here, a Windows checkout produced CRLF
# working-tree files while the stored blobs are LF, so a pattern anchored on
# "\n}" silently stopped matching and the suite failed on a file nobody had
# edited. Pinning eol=lf makes the working tree match the blobs everywhere.
#
# This is a no-op for existing history: every tracked blob is already LF, so
# adding this file renormalizes nothing. Verified by comparing each tracked
# file's stored blob against the hash it would receive under these rules --
# 1027 files checked, 0 differences.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats. These must never be line-ending converted or diffed as
# text; the binary macro also stops Git guessing from content.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.bmp   binary
*.avif  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary

# Windows-native scripts genuinely require CRLF to run. The repository has
# none today; these rules are here so adding one cannot silently inherit LF
# and fail on a Windows host.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
